// RFC 0001: capabilities and effects.
site {
  rfcs {
    capabilities : Rfc {
      Title: {"Capabilities and effects"}
      Description: {"A function's signature says what it may touch. Effects on the surface, capabilities underneath; pure code is cacheable by construction."}
      Number: {1}
      (DependsOn, site.charter)

      Lede summary("In the new edition, a function's signature says what it may touch: the filesystem, the network, the clock, randomness. You write effects; the compiler passes capabilities. A function that names none is pure, so blaze can cache it, sandbox it and run it sync or async without being told.")

      Section motivation(title: "Motivation") {
        P today("Any Rust function can open a file, read the clock or call into C, and nothing in its signature says so. That costs blaze three things it wants.")
        Ul costs {
          Li caching("**Caching.** The [[fn_cache]] can reuse a result only when the function cannot see anything but its arguments. Today it proves that by refusing statics, foreign calls, address exposure and the like, one rule at a time, and asks for `#[blaze::cache]`. A signature that says *pure* would make the check one line and the attribute unnecessary.")
          Li sandbox("**Sandboxing.** Build scripts and proc macros run with the user's full authority ([[sandbox]]). With capabilities, a build script gets read access to its crate and write access to `OUT_DIR`, and can do nothing else.")
          Li colour("**Sync and async.** Rust writes the same logic twice, once blocking and once `async`, and libraries pick a runtime for you. If I/O is a capability passed in, the caller chooses how it runs.")
        }
        P testing("It also makes testing honest: a test passes an in-memory filesystem and a fixed clock instead of mocking globals.")
      }

      Section design(title: "Design sketch") {
        P surface("On the surface, effects: after the return type, `/` lists what a function uses, as in Effekt (`T / Fs`, read \"T, using Fs\"). Several join with `+`, like trait bounds. Syntax is illustrative; see the open issues.") { (Cites, refs.koka); (Cites, refs.effekt) }
        Code example(lang: "rust", text: `// a crate on the new edition
        fn load_config(path: &Path) -> Result<Config, Error> / Fs \{
            let text = fs::read_to_string(path)?;
            parse(&text)
        }

        // No \`/\`: pure. blaze may cache it, and does.
        fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<Config, Error> \{ /* ... */ }

        fn main() / Fs + Net + Clock \{
            let config = load_config("app.toml".as_ref())?;
            // ...
        }`)
        P underneath("Underneath, capabilities: each effect is a hidden parameter. `/ Fs` lowers to a generic parameter bounded by a trait, so `load_config` takes an `F: Fs` and calls through it. Generic, not `dyn`: each caller's choice is monomorphised and inlined, with no vtable on the hot path.") { (Cites, refs.cap_std); (Cites, refs.effekt) }
        Code lowered(lang: "rust", text: `fn load_config<F: Fs>(fs: &F, path: &Path) -> Result<Config, Error> \{
            let text = fs.read_to_string(path)?;
            parse(&text)
        }`)
        P handlers("A caller that has a capability passes it on implicitly; one that wants to narrow or replace it says so. A test runs the same code against an in-memory filesystem.")
        Code test(lang: "rust", text: `#[test]
        fn reads_defaults() \{
            let fs = MemFs::from([("app.toml", "port = 80")]);
            with fs \{
                assert_eq!(load_config("app.toml".as_ref()).unwrap().port, 80);
            }
        }`)
        P main("`main` receives every capability the platform has. Nothing else is ambient: a function that names no effects cannot reach one, except through `unsafe` (see the open issues).")
      }

      Section sync_async(title: "Sync and async from one source") {
        P zig("Zig 0.16 made the same choice from the other side: I/O is an `Io` value passed in, and the caller picks a blocking or an evented implementation. It dropped the `async` and `await` keywords, and code is no longer coloured by how its I/O runs.") { (Cites, refs.zig_016); (Cites, refs.kelley_io); (Cites, refs.cro_io); (Cites, refs.zig_6025) }
        P runtime("Zig's `Io` is a runtime interface: a vtable. An evented implementation needs a stack per task, or stackless frames, which Zig does not have yet; the proposals for them are still open.") { (Cites, refs.zig_23446); (Cites, refs.zig_23367); (Cites, refs.lwn_zig) }
        P ours("blaze owns the compiler, so it can decide at compile time. A function that uses an I/O capability is compiled twice when needed: a direct version, which blocks, and a stackless state machine like the one `async fn` lowers to today. Which one a call gets follows from the capability the caller passes, the way a generic picks its instance. No keywords in the source, no stacks per task, and no function colouring.")
        P sans_io("The library side is the sans-IO pattern made a rule: protocol code that never performs I/O itself, driven by whoever holds the capabilities.") { (Cites, refs.sans_io); (Cites, refs.firezone); (Cites, refs.quinn) }
      }

      Section buys(title: "What it buys") {
        Ul list {
          Li cache("Automatic caching: a function with no effects, no `unsafe` and only pure callees is eligible, with no attribute. One with `/ Clock` or `/ Rand` is not, visibly.")
          Li sandbox("Sandboxed build scripts and proc macros, granted only what they need.")
          Li one_source("One source for blocking and async code; the caller picks.")
          Li tests("Tests without global mocks: pass a fake capability.")
          Li review("Review: a diff that adds `/ Net` to a parser is visible in the signature, not buried in a call three levels down.")
        }
      }

      Section prior(title: "Prior art") {
        Ul list {
          Li zig("[[zig_016]], [[kelley_io]], [[cro_io]], [[zig_25592]]: I/O as an interface the caller passes.")
          Li cap_std("[[cap_std]]: capability-based filesystem and network APIs for today's Rust.")
          Li sans_io("[[sans_io]] and [[firezone]]: protocols that never do I/O themselves.")
          Li koka("[[koka]] and [[effekt]]: typed effects and handlers, compiled without a runtime for effects; Effekt treats effects as capabilities.")
          Li unison("[[unison]]: abilities in signatures, and pure code identified and cached by hash ([[unison_idea]]).")
          Li scala("[[scala_cc]]: types that track which capabilities a value captures.")
          Li wasi("[[wasi]]: the host grants capabilities to a wasm module; nothing is ambient.")
          Li ocaml("[[ocaml_fx]]: untyped effect handlers on fibers, the stackful alternative.")
          Li rust("[[effects_init]]: the Rust project's work on effect generics (`const`, `async`).")
        }
      }

      Section issues(title: "Open issues") {
        Issue granularity(title: "How fine are capabilities?") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("`Fs` or `FsRead` and `FsWrite`? A directory, like cap-std's `Dir`, or the whole filesystem? Finer is more checkable and more to write; the charter says to pay for checkability, but only for guarantees someone relies on.")
        }
        Issue old_editions(title: "Calling old-edition code") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Code in editions 2015 to 2024 has ambient authority: it may do anything. Calling it from the new edition needs a capability that means everything (`/ Ambient`?), which makes caching and sandboxing impossible through it. How much of std and crates.io can be given precise effects, and how?")
        }
        Issue inference(title: "Inference and semver") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Must every function spell out its effects, or can private functions infer them? Inferred effects on public functions would make adding a log line a breaking change; explicit ones are noise. One option: explicit at crate boundaries, inferred inside.")
        }
        Issue dynamic(title: "Trait objects and function pointers") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("A `dyn Fn` or `fn()` hides what it uses. Effects have to be part of the type (`dyn Fn() / Fs`), and a pure `fn()` must not be able to hold an effectful one.")
        }
        Issue code_size(title: "Two versions of every I/O function") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Compiling a direct and a stackless version doubles code for effectful functions that are used both ways. Only instantiate what is called, as generics do; measure what that costs real programs.")
        }
        Issue panics(title: "Is panicking an effect?") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Almost everything can panic. Treating it as an effect would be accurate and unusable. Likely answer: panics are not tracked, but a cached function that panics is not cached.")
        }
        Issue alloc(title: "Is allocation an effect?") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Proposed: explicit everywhere, as in Zig. Allocation is a capability like `Fs`: a function that allocates says `/ Alloc`, and the caller chooses which allocator it gets, so a request can run on an arena, a frame on a bump allocator, a test on a counting allocator. Code with no `Alloc` cannot allocate at all, which is what real-time audio callbacks, interrupt handlers and kernel paths need and what nothing checks today.") { (Cites, refs.kelley_io) }
          P cache("Allocation does not make a function impure. A function with only `/ Alloc` gives the same answer for the same input; allocation is observable only through failure and through addresses, and the cache already refuses functions that expose addresses. So `Alloc` decides which allocator and whether any, not whether a result can be cached.")
          P cost("The cost is that almost everything says `/ Alloc`, and std's collections take their allocator (`Vec<T, A>`, as in Rust's unstable allocator API, or Zig's unmanaged containers that take it per call). Open: per collection or per call, how code in old editions gets one (the global allocator, passed in at the edition boundary), and whether `Alloc` is inferred inside a crate and only written at public items.")
        }
        Issue threads(title: "Threads and Send") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("Spawning is an effect. Can a capability cross threads? Some (`Clock`) obviously; some (a single-threaded executor's handle) not. This is `Send` again, for capabilities.")
        }
        Issue unsafe_ffi(title: "unsafe and FFI") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("`unsafe` code and `extern` calls can do anything. A function that uses them either declares the effects it really has (trusted, like `unsafe impl Send`) or counts as ambient.")
        }
        Issue escape(title: "Capabilities in cached values") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("A pure function must not return or capture a capability, or its cached result would carry authority from one run into another. Capability types must be non-cacheable, and the check has to see through generics.")
        }
        Issue syntax(title: "Syntax") {
          (Status, Open)
          P q("`/ Fs` after the return type (Effekt's form, the current sketch), `uses Fs`, an attribute, or bounds on a hidden generic? `/` reads well and is short, but needs checking against division in const generic and return-type positions. `with fs \{ ... }` for handlers, or ordinary arguments? Pick whatever reads best in diffs; nothing is decided.")
        }
      }

      References refs
    }
  }
}
